WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.080
Forrest Gump was a big-time crowd-pleaser,
and a sequel would've definitely also earned

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.800
stacks of cash at the box office.

00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:10.040
So what kept a potential part two from ever
hitting theaters?

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.360
Well, stop eating those shrimp and grab your
running shoes as we find out why Forrest Gump

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.960
2 never got made.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.440
Once upon a time, Tom Hanks was best known
as a comedian.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.600
But then the Oscar-winning double whammy of
Philadelphia and Forrest Gump turned him into

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.600
an esteemed actor and gave him serious Hollywood
clout.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.480
Following those back-to-back Lead Actor wins,
Hanks could've easily made a Gump sequel,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.890
but instead, he opted for Apollo 13.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.540
So why didn't he revisit his iconic character?

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.920
In 1995, Hanks said that he didn't think Forrest
Gump 2 was a good idea.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.820
For one thing, as he told Entertainment Weekly,
he was tired of all those catchphrases.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.340
But more importantly, he was worried a sequel
would hurt the legacy of the original film.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.220
As he put it,

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.050
"I have to confess I don't see this a franchise.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:55.920
A sequel would ruin what we had done.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.540
It would be like Jaws 2."

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.990
While Forrest Gump might seem like a distinctly
cinematic experience, the movie was actually

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.020
based on a novel by Winston Groom.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.920
So when it came time to adapt the story for
the big screen, Paramount tapped Eric Roth

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.960
to write the screenplay.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.960
Roth is an incredibly respected writer who
won an Academy Award for his Gump script and

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.190
went on to nab even more Oscar nominations
for the likes of The Curious Case of Benjamin

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.860
Button and A Star Is Born.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.450
And as it turns out, in between writing all
these acclaimed screenplays, Roth was busy

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.020
hammering out a script for a Forrest Gump
sequel.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:33.020
For a brief moment, it looked like we might
return to the world of Lieutenant Dan and

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.360
the Bubba Gump Shrimp Company.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.400
But while Roth is a fantastic writer who's
produced some spectacular stories, his Forrest

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.049
Gump 2 script might not be his most... mainstream
work.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.920
Forrest Gump is a movie that wants you to
cry.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.700
And the saddest scene in the entire film is
probably when Jenny dies, leaving Forrest

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.780
absolutely wrecked.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:57.420
Played by Robin Wright, Jenny has lived a
pretty rough life, and she eventually contracts

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.800
a mysterious virus that doctors can't cure.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.000
It is implied that she succumbed to AIDS,
and that was going to play a big role in the

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:04.900
aborted sequel.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.980
According to Eric Roth, Gump and his son now
lived in Florida, and everyone in his community

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.799
was going to freak out when they learned Forrest
Jr. contracted AIDS from his mom.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.020
As Roth noted to Consequence of Sound,

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:22.180
"We had a funny sequence where they were [desegregating]
busing in Florida at the same time, so people

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.090
were angry about either the busing, or [their]
kids having to go to school with the kid who

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:26.960
had AIDS.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.320
So there was a big conflict."

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:33.260
So according to Roth, Forrest Gump 2 was going
to use racism and the AIDS crisis together

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.900
for the sake of a comedy bit.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.799
Those are two highly sensitive topics that
most moviegoers would probably agree aren't

00:02:39.799 --> 00:02:41.059
the best fodder for humor.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:47.409
In addition to the phenomenal acting and nostalgic
soundtrack, Forrest Gump is also praised for

00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:48.540
its visual effects.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.020
The folks at Industrial Light &amp; Magic actually
won an Oscar for their incredible work.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.150
Perhaps their most impressive accomplishment
was putting Tom Hanks into archival footage

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.060
alongside famous historical figures like Richard
Nixon and John F. Kennedy.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:06.220
Eric Roth planned on having Forrest encounter
even more famous figures in the sequel, including

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.500
an iconic cameo that doesn't sound like a
great fit.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.020
Evidently, Forrest was going to be very involved
in the saga of O.J. Simpson.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.519
Somehow, he would be the one responsible for
tossing the bloody glove found at Simpson's

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.560
Brentwood home.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.529
Stranger still, Forrest would somehow wind
up in the back of Simpson's white Bronco as

00:03:24.529 --> 00:03:28.020
the football star leads the police on the
slowest car chase in history.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.099
The O.J. Simpson trial was obviously a huge
news story in the '90s, but it would probably

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:35.989
feel bizarrely out of place in a Forrest Gump
sequel.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.880
For all its happy moments, Forrest Gump is
ultimately a tragedy.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.740
After all, it ends just moments after Forrest
gives a tearful goodbye at Jenny's grave.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.660
As for the sequel, Eric Roth had no intention
of giving our chocolate-loving hero a happy

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:51.160
ending.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:56.520
Speaking with Yahoo, Roth explained that Forrest
would have a new love interest: a Native American

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.220
woman he meets while riding a bus.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.160
She was going to be a nursery school teacher,
and every day, Forrest would sit on a park

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.879
bench and share a lunch break with her.

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:06.840
But she wouldn't be teaching at any old nursery
school.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:12.479
Instead, it would be located inside the federal
building in Oklahoma City where in 1995 Timothy

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.940
McVeigh set off a truck full of explosives.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.620
Forrest's girlfriend was going to die in the
blast.

00:04:17.629 --> 00:04:21.099
Worse still, it would happen while Forrest
was sitting on that bench, as the building

00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:24.440
explodes right behind him, which sounds more
than a little tasteless.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:30.240
So if Eric Roth had a script ready to go,
how come Forrest Gump 2 never got made?

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.560
It turns out that he turned in his final draft
on September 10, 2001.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.080
The very next day, the world was turned upside
down.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:42.180
The 9/11 terrorist attacks impacted the production
of quite a few films, and it stopped the Gump

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.940
sequel from even getting past the screenplay
phase.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.639
After all, the movie was supposed to end with
Forrest's girlfriend dying in one of the most

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.120
devastating terrorist attacks in American
history.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.120
And that just wasn't going to fly in the aftermath
of 9/11.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.500
So on the day of the attacks, he met up with
Tom Hanks and director Robert Zemeckis to

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.080
commiserate.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.430
"We looked at each other and said, 'This movie
has no meaning anymore.'

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.200
In that sense."

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.900
So it's probably for the best that Forrest
Gump 2 never happened.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:12.110
From what we know of the screenplay, it doesn't
like it would've been anywhere near as beloved

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.180
as the original.

